FYI here's a quick benchmark showing how this lowers the overhead of methods like `Regex.IsMatch`.

``` ini

BenchmarkDotNet=v0.13.1, OS=Windows 10.0.19044.1645 (21H2)
Intel Core i7-6700 CPU 3.40GHz (Skylake), 1 CPU, 8 logical and 4 physical cores
.NET SDK=7.0.100-preview.5.22256.6
  [Host]     : .NET 7.0.0 (7.0.22.25515), X64 RyuJIT
  Job-QZBIHU : .NET 7.0.0 (42.42.42.42424), X64 RyuJIT
  Job-MPMWTW : .NET 7.0.0 (42.42.42.42424), X64 RyuJIT


```
|  Toolchain |     Mean |    Error |   StdDev | Ratio | RatioSD |
|----------- |---------:|---------:|---------:|------:|--------:|
|       main | 80.51 ns | 1.646 ns | 4.392 ns |  1.00 |    0.00 |
| regex_perf | 69.44 ns | 1.154 ns | 1.079 ns |  0.86 |    0.05 |


```cs
public partial class RegexRunner
{
    [RegexGenerator("[A-Fa-f0-9]{8}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{12}", RegexOptions.ExplicitCapture)]
    private static partial Regex MyRegex();

    [Benchmark]
    [ArgumentsSource(nameof(IsMatchArguments))]
    public bool IsMatch(string input)
    {
        return MyRegex().IsMatch(input);
    }

    public static IEnumerable<object> IsMatchArguments()
    {
        yield return "db9119ab-b0be-41cc-98b8-825374117ace";
    }
}
```

This benchmark was based on the quick prototype at https://github.com/GrabYourPitchforks/runtime/commit/2bf3adeb3bd005f541806507a9a422ddca99e72d. From playing around with some measurements, it looks like half the improvement was from changes within the _Regex_ type itself, while half was from changing how _Interlocked.Exchange_ operates.
